  Design Brief: Sliding-Block Escape Puzzle

  Working title: Finding Temo

  Concept. A grid-based sliding-block puzzle. The player frees a key-bearing tile by clearing a path to an exit gap in
  the border wall. One-screen levels, no timer, pure spatial reasoning.

  Core loop. Read the board → identify the blocking chain → move obstructions in dependency order → slide the key out →
  next level. Sessions are 30–90 seconds per puzzle.

  Rules.

  Grid sizes 4×4 to 7×7, fixed per level.
  Pieces are 1×1, 1×2, or 1×3 rectangles.
  Rectangles slide only along their long axis; 1×1 pieces move freely. Nothing rotates.
  Drag moves a piece as far as the player pulls it, stopping at the first collision. A move counts as one action
  regardless of distance.
  The key tile is 1×2 horizontal. Exit is a gap in the wall aligned to one row.

  Progression. 10 hand-tuned or solver-verified levels.(ability to add more levels should be key) Difficulty scales by
  piece count, then by dependency depth (how many moves must precede the first useful one), not by grid size alone.

  Futuere support for: dead-end decoys, pieces that must move away from the exit, exits on the left or top edge

